      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2020676300508393506",
      "url": "https://x.com/Ignis_Rex/status/2020676300508393506",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/Ignis_Rex/status/2020676300508393506",
      "text": "American Robotaxi are actually driven by Pinoys working in Call Centers in the Philippines!\n\nThe Robotaxi Paradox: How \"Autonomous\" Driving Could Offshore American Jobs and National Security\n\nIn a hearing that might have seemed plucked from a dystopian tech thriller, Waymo, Google's self-driving car company, recently admitted what many suspected but few acknowledged: those \"driverless\" robotaxis navigating American streets aren't quite as autonomous as advertised. When stumped by a construction cone or an ambiguous lane marker, these vehicles phone a friend – often a \"fleet response agent\" working in a call center thousands of miles away, in the Philippines.\nThis revelation isn't just a minor technical footnote; it's a flashing red light for anyone concerned about American jobs, data sovereignty, and national security. The robotaxi paradox reveals a dangerous double-standard: we're told automation will revolutionize transportation, yet the human \"safety net\" is quietly being outsourced to the cheapest possible labor pool, while simultaneously denying Americans the very jobs these futuristic vehicles were supposed to create (or at least, augment).\n\nLet's be clear: this isn't simply about tech companies seeking efficiency. This is about critical infrastructure, American data, and the integrity of our streets.\n\n*The \"Autonomous\" Lie: A Blow to American Workers\nThe promise of autonomous vehicles has always included the vision of freeing up human labor for more skilled tasks. Yet, Waymo's admission shatters that illusion. The jobs of monitoring, guiding, and troubleshooting these vehicles – roles that require human judgment and intervention – are not disappearing. They're just being exported.\nWhile Silicon Valley touts innovation, the reality for an American worker is stark: the opportunity to be a remote \"fleet response agent\" or even an in-car safety driver is increasingly being shipped overseas. This isn't innovation creating new opportunities at home; it's innovation creating new avenues for offshoring, undercutting the domestic labor market, and adding another layer to the gig economy, albeit one conducted from a different continent. It’s a bitter pill for American workers struggling in an economy increasingly shaped by global competition.\n\n*Data Sovereignty: Who's Watching Our Streets?\nThis brings us to a far more insidious threat: data sovereignty. Robotaxis are not just vehicles; they are rolling data collection platforms. Equipped with an array of cameras, LiDAR, and sensors, they capture a real-time, high-definition stream of our streets, our homes, our businesses, and our daily lives. Every pedestrian, every street sign, every military base entrance, every private residence is recorded and analyzed.\n\nNow, imagine these live data feeds being accessed by operators in a foreign call center. While Waymo assures us these agents merely provide \"guidance,\" the very act of providing that guidance necessitates access to incredibly granular, real-time visual and environmental data. How can we ensure the integrity and security of this data when it traverses international borders and is viewed by individuals outside U.S. jurisdiction?\n\nThe potential for foreign intelligence agencies to exploit these remote access points is not a far-fetched conspiracy theory; it’s a legitimate national security concern. The \"eyes\" of our future transportation system could, in effect, be operating under the scrutiny of foreign powers. This creates an undeniable vulnerability, especially for a nation that consistently champions data privacy and security.\n\n*National Security: The \"Physical Takeover\" Threat\nFinally, there’s the chilling prospect of a \"physical takeover.\" While Waymo states remote agents don't directly \"drive\" the cars, they influence critical decision-making in complex scenarios. What if a hostile state were to compromise these overseas call centers? What if the \"guidance\" provided was intentionally malicious, designed to cause disruption, gridlock, or even direct harm?\n\nSenator Markey's concerns about \"transatlantic backseat drivers\" are not merely rhetorical. The latency inherent in transmitting data across oceans, coupled with the potential for miscommunication or, worse, deliberate subversion, introduces a systemic risk into our domestic transportation network. A foreign entity could, theoretically, influence the movement of thousands of vehicles on American soil, impacting everything from emergency services to military logistics.\n\nIt's Time for a Reckoning\nThe allure of \"autonomous\" technology should not blind us to the foundational principles of American economic security and national defense. We must demand greater transparency from companies deploying these technologies. We need robust regulations that ensure critical decision-making and data access for our transportation infrastructure remain firmly within U.S. borders, creating jobs for Americans rather than exporting them.\n\nThe robotaxi paradox isn't just a technical glitch; it's a wake-up call. If we allow our streets to be \"driven\" by remote workers in foreign lands, while simultaneously denying those jobs to Americans and compromising our data, we're not advancing into the future – we're building a potentially dangerous and inequitable one. \n\nWhat happened if the job is secretly outsourced to N Korea because they are the cheapest and most efficient?\n\nIt's time for a serious conversation about what \"Made in America\" truly means in the age of AI and robotaxis.\n\nhttps://t.co/DCTUmrP97g",
